  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "If $kW=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhere $W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To express $kW,$ we multiply each entry of $W$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kW & =k[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kW$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the  leftmost elements of the third row, $2k$ and $10.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =10 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements.",
